            <ArticleTitle>[Spontaneous ascitic infection in cirrhotic Africans. Descriptive study apropos of 12 cases].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>319-21</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Spontaneous ascitic infection (SAI) is a frequent and serious complication of cirrhosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">In a retrospective study, the authors report clinical and biological data associated with SAI for cirrhotic patients in an African medical centre.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Twenty-two cirrhotic patients with ascites were included in a one-year study (November 1996 to October 1997). Clinical and biological data were obtained through medical files.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean age of the 22 cirrhotic patients with ascites (12 men, 10 women) was 48.9 years. Twelve cases of SAI were found. In a univariate analysis, the more frequent data in patients with SAI when compared to patients without SAI were: fever or hypothermia (91.7% versus 10%, p = 0.002), abdominal pain (83.3% versus 40%, p = 0.046), cloudy ascitic fluid (66.7% versus 10%, p = 0.003), medium albuminemia (18.2 g/l versus 23 g/l, p = 0.02), medium prothrombin rate (42.8% versus 58.3%; p = 0.04) and ascitic fluid protein level &lt; or = 10 g/l (91.7% versus 30%, p = 0.01). The protein level in ascitic fluid cirrhotic patients was significantly lower in SAI than in patients without SAI (7.6 g/l versus 11 g/l; p = 0.005). In a multivariate analysis, protein levels in ascitic fluid were the only factor associated with SAI (p = 0.024).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
